{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-64333",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c\\times f(t)\\,dt & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =(i+k)\\times (i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{2}{3})i-(1-1)j+(\\frac{2}{3}-0)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
